(self.CURRICULUM ||= { lessons: [] }).lessons.push(...[{"n": 59, "title": "Lesson 59: Difficult words: 어쩌면, 아무래도, 가꾸다, 연세, 뵈다, 차림, 즉", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-3-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-3-lessons-59-66/lesson-59/", "vocab": [{"ko": "어쩌면", "en": "maybe, perhaps", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "아무래도", "en": "probably", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "가꾸다", "en": "to grow (plants); to groom oneself", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "연세", "en": "age (honorific)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "뵈다", "en": "to see (high respect)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "차림", "en": "attire, dressed in", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "옷차림", "en": "outfit", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "잠옷", "en": "pajamas", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "운동복", "en": "exercise clothes", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "즉", "en": "that is, in other words", "pos": "adverb"}], "grammar": [{"point": "어쩌면 (maybe / how on earth)", "explanation": "어쩌면 is an adverb that usually goes at the start of a sentence. Its main use is 'maybe' or 'perhaps': the speaker is guessing and isn't sure. 어쩌면 can't carry the guess on its own, so it's paired with an ending that also expresses possibility, most often ~ㄹ/을지도 모르다 ('might').\n\nForming ~ㄹ/을지도 모르다: after a vowel stem add ㄹ지도 모르다 (오다 → 오 + ㄹ지도 → 올지도 몰라요). After a consonant stem add 을지도 모르다 (있다 → 있 + 을지도 → 있을지도 몰라요). ㄹ stems keep their ㄹ and add 지도 (알다 → 알지도 몰라요). ㅂ irregulars change ㅂ to 우 (쉽다 → 쉬울지도 몰라요).\n\nIts second use is in a question, often with 이렇게 or 그렇게. Here it shows amazement or admiration: 'how on earth...?' or 'how did you manage to...?'. It isn't really asking for an answer. Tell the two apart by context: a guess about something uncertain means 'maybe'; an amazed question about something someone has done or can do means 'how on earth'.", "examples": [{"ko": "어쩌면 내일 비가 올지도 몰라요.", "en": "It might rain tomorrow."}, {"ko": "어쩌면 다음 주에 할아버지를 뵐 수 있을지도 몰라요.", "en": "Maybe I'll be able to see Grandfather next week."}, {"ko": "어쩌면 정원을 이렇게 예쁘게 가꿨어요?", "en": "How did you manage to make the garden this pretty?"}, {"ko": "어쩌면 동생이 집에 있을지도 몰라요.", "en": "My younger sibling might be at home."}, {"ko": "어쩌면 이번 시험은 생각보다 쉬울지도 몰라요.", "en": "This exam might be easier than we think.", "note": "쉽다 is ㅂ irregular: 쉽 → 쉬우 + ㄹ지도 → 쉬울지도."}, {"ko": "어쩌면 이렇게 한국어를 잘하세요?", "en": "How on earth do you speak Korean so well?"}]}, {"point": "아무래도 + ~ㄹ/을 것 같다 (probably)", "explanation": "아무래도 means 'probably' or 'it looks like', with a nuance: the speaker has thought it over, maybe even hoped for a different answer, and still reaches the same conclusion. It feels like 'no matter how I look at it...'. The conclusion is often a little unwelcome: being late, rain, having to cancel.\n\n아무래도 is just an adverb; the guess itself comes from the ending. For a future or general guess, use ~ㄹ/을 것 같다: 오다 → 오 + ㄹ 것 같다 → 올 것 같아요; 늦다 → 늦 + 을 것 같다 → 늦을 것 같아요. For something that already happened, use ~ㄴ/은 것 같다 on a verb: 떠나다 → 떠난 것 같아요; 먹다 → 먹은 것 같아요. For an adjective describing the present, ~ㄴ/은 것 같다 is present: 작다 → 작은 것 같아요.\n\nCompare with 어쩌면 (maybe, unsure). 아무래도 is much more confident: 어쩌면 비가 올지도 몰라요 = 'it might rain'; 아무래도 비가 올 것 같아요 = 'I'm pretty sure it's going to rain'.", "examples": [{"ko": "아무래도 오늘은 늦을 것 같아요.", "en": "It looks like I'll probably be late today."}, {"ko": "아무래도 그 사람이 벌써 떠난 것 같아요.", "en": "He has probably already left."}, {"ko": "아무래도 비가 올 것 같아요.", "en": "It will probably rain."}, {"ko": "아무래도 이 신발은 저한테 작은 것 같아요.", "en": "These shoes seem to be too small for me."}, {"ko": "아무래도 내일 모임에 못 갈 것 같아요.", "en": "I probably won't be able to make it to tomorrow's gathering."}, {"ko": "아무래도 길을 잘못 든 것 같아요.", "en": "I think we probably took a wrong turn.", "note": "들다 is an ㄹ stem, so the ㄹ drops before ㄴ: 들 + ㄴ → 든."}]}, {"point": "Honorific words: 연세 and 뵈다/뵙다", "explanation": "Korean has special respectful words for people you look up to: grandparents, older strangers, teachers, a boss. 연세 replaces 나이 (age). Ask with 어떻게 되다 plus honorific 시: 연세가 어떻게 되세요? Use 나이 for friends, children and yourself; never call your own age 연세.\n\n뵈다 and 뵙다 replace 보다 when you see or meet someone you must respect. They are humble words: they lower you, the person doing the seeing, so you don't add 시 to them. 뵈다 conjugates like 되다: 뵈 + 어요 → 뵈어요 → 봬요 (just like 되어요 → 돼요). Past: 뵈 + 었어요 → 뵀어요. Before consonant endings the stem stays 뵈: 뵈러, 뵐 거예요.\n\n뵙다 means the same but is more formal. It is used mostly with consonant endings: 뵙겠습니다, 뵙고. 처음 뵙겠습니다 is the standard formal greeting when you first meet someone. A common spelling mistake is 뵈요: 뵈 can't take 요 directly, so the correct form is 봬요. A quick test: wherever 되다 would become 돼, 뵈다 becomes 봬.", "examples": [{"ko": "할머니께서는 연세가 어떻게 되세요?", "en": "How old is your grandmother?"}, {"ko": "내일 선생님을 뵈러 갈 거예요.", "en": "I'm going to go see my teacher tomorrow."}, {"ko": "다음 주에 봬요.", "en": "See you next week."}, {"ko": "처음 뵙겠습니다.", "en": "Nice to meet you."}, {"ko": "어제 오랜만에 할아버지를 뵀어요.", "en": "Yesterday I saw my grandfather for the first time in a while.", "note": "뵈 + 었어요 → 뵈었어요 → 뵀어요."}, {"ko": "할아버지 연세가 올해 여든이세요.", "en": "My grandfather turns eighty this year."}, {"ko": "사장님을 뵙고 싶어요.", "en": "I'd like to see the company president."}]}, {"point": "가꾸다 (to grow / to groom)", "explanation": "가꾸다 means looking after something regularly and carefully so that it grows well or looks nice. With plants, 정원 (garden), 채소 or 꽃 it means 'to grow' or 'to tend'. With 외모 (appearance), 몸, 피부 or 머리 it means 'to take care of how you look'. It describes ongoing care, so it often appears with ~고 있다 or words like 요즘 and 열심히.\n\nConjugation: the stem 가꾸 ends in ㅜ, so it takes 어요, and ㅜ + ㅓ contracts to ㅝ: 가꾸 + 어요 → 가꾸어요 → 가꿔요. Past: 가꾸 + 었어요 → 가꿨어요. Consonant endings attach directly: 가꾸고, 가꿀 거예요, 가꾸려고.\n\nIt overlaps with 키우다 ('to raise, to grow'), which is about making something get bigger: children, pets, plants. 가꾸다 is about tending and keeping something looking good, and only 가꾸다 works for your own appearance.", "examples": [{"ko": "아버지는 마당에서 채소를 가꿔요.", "en": "My father grows vegetables in the yard."}, {"ko": "요즘 운동하면서 몸을 가꾸고 있어요.", "en": "These days I'm getting in shape by exercising."}, {"ko": "데이트 전에 외모를 가꿔야 돼요.", "en": "I need to fix myself up before the date."}, {"ko": "할머니께서 꽃을 정말 예쁘게 가꾸셨어요.", "en": "My grandmother tended her flowers really beautifully.", "note": "Honorific past: 가꾸 + 시 + 었어요 → 가꾸셨어요."}, {"ko": "피부를 가꾸려고 물을 많이 마셔요.", "en": "I drink a lot of water to take care of my skin."}, {"ko": "내년에는 작은 정원을 가꾸고 싶어요.", "en": "Next year I want to tend a small garden."}]}, {"point": "Noun + 차림 (dressed in ...)", "explanation": "차림 means the way someone is dressed. Put a clothing noun in front of it to say someone is wearing that, usually when the outfit is worth mentioning for the situation: 잠옷 차림 (in pajamas), 운동복 차림 (in workout clothes).\n\nTo say someone did something dressed that way, add (으)로. 차림 ends in a consonant, so it takes 으로: 잠옷 차림 + 으로 → 잠옷 차림으로 나갔어요. You can also finish a sentence with 이다: 운동복 차림이에요 ('He's in workout clothes').\n\n옷차림 (옷 + 차림) is the general word for an outfit or style of dress and takes ordinary particles: 옷차림이 멋있어요. 차림 is about the overall look, not the act of dressing; to say 'put on' or 'wear', you still use 입다.", "examples": [{"ko": "동생이 잠옷 차림으로 밖에 나갔어요.", "en": "My brother went outside in his pajamas."}, {"ko": "그는 운동복 차림으로 학교에 왔어요.", "en": "He came to school in workout clothes."}, {"ko": "오늘 옷차림이 정말 멋있어요.", "en": "Your outfit today is really nice."}, {"ko": "아버지는 항상 편한 옷차림을 좋아하세요.", "en": "My father always likes comfortable clothes."}, {"ko": "왜 아직도 잠옷 차림이에요?", "en": "Why are you still in your pajamas?"}, {"ko": "가벼운 옷차림으로 오세요.", "en": "Please come dressed in light clothes.", "note": "가볍다 is ㅂ irregular: 가볍 → 가벼우 + ㄴ → 가벼운."}]}, {"point": "즉 (that is / in other words)", "explanation": "즉 means 'that is' or 'in other words', like 'i.e.' in English. Use it to say what you just said again in a clearer or more exact way, or to spell out what it plainly means. It sounds a little formal and precise; in casual speech people often use 그러니까 instead.\n\n즉 can sit in two places. Inside a sentence, it links two nouns that refer to the same thing: A, 즉 B (제 아버지의 아버지, 즉 제 할아버지). Between sentences, it starts a new sentence that sums up or explains the one before: 그는 연락이 없어요. 즉 오지 않을 거예요.\n\nWhatever follows 즉 should restate or spell out what came before, not add something new. To add a separate fact use 그리고; to give a result or consequence, 그래서 usually sounds more natural.", "examples": [{"ko": "그분은 제 아버지의 아버지, 즉 제 할아버지예요.", "en": "He is my father's father, in other words my grandfather."}, {"ko": "시험은 다음 주 월요일, 즉 3일에 있어요.", "en": "The exam is next Monday, that is, on the 3rd."}, {"ko": "그는 연락이 없어요. 즉 오지 않을 거예요.", "en": "He hasn't been in touch. In other words, he won't come."}, {"ko": "모레, 즉 수요일에 만나요.", "en": "Let's meet the day after tomorrow, that is, on Wednesday."}, {"ko": "그 식당은 일요일에 쉬어요. 즉 오늘은 문을 닫았어요.", "en": "That restaurant is closed on Sundays. In other words, it's closed today."}, {"ko": "이 일은 두 시간, 즉 백이십 분이 걸려요.", "en": "This work takes two hours, that is, 120 minutes."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "어쩌면 내일 눈이 ___ 몰라요.", "answer": "올지도", "options": ["올지도", "오지도", "와지도", "올까도"], "en": "It might snow tomorrow.", "why": "어쩌면 pairs with ~ㄹ/을지도 모르다, and 오다 takes ㄹ지도 because it ends in a vowel."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "___ 오늘은 집에 늦게 갈 것 같아요.", "answer": "아무래도", "options": ["아무래도", "즉", "아무도", "아무거나"], "en": "After thinking it over, I'll probably go home late today.", "why": "아무래도 + ~ㄹ/을 것 같다 means 'probably', after weighing things up."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "할아버지는 ___가 어떻게 되세요?", "answer": "연세", "options": ["연세", "살", "년", "해"], "en": "How old is your grandfather?", "why": "연세 is the respectful word for age used for elderly people."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "내일 선생님을 ___ 갈 거예요.", "answer": "뵈러", "options": ["뵈러", "봬러", "뵈으러", "뵈서"], "en": "I'm going to go see my teacher tomorrow.", "why": "The stem 뵈 takes 러 directly; 봬 is only the contraction of 뵈어."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "선생님, 다음 주에 ___.", "answer": "봬요", "options": ["봬요", "뵈요", "뵈다요", "뵙어요"], "en": "See you next week, teacher.", "why": "뵈 + 어요 contracts to 봬요, just like 되 + 어요 becomes 돼요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "어머니는 집 앞에서 작은 정원을 ___.", "answer": "가꿔요", "options": ["가꿔요", "가꾸요", "가꺼요", "가까요"], "en": "My mother tends a small garden in front of the house.", "why": "가꾸 + 어요 merges into 가꿔요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "동생이 잠옷 ___ 편의점에 갔어요.", "answer": "차림으로", "options": ["차림으로", "차림을", "차림이", "차림에서"], "en": "My younger sibling went to the convenience store in pajamas.", "why": "Clothing + 차림으로 says what someone is dressed in while doing something."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "제 생일은 다음 주 금요일, ___ 10일이에요.", "answer": "즉", "options": ["즉", "아무래도", "어쩌면", "하지만"], "en": "My birthday is next Friday, that is, the 10th.", "why": "즉 introduces a restatement: 'that is' or 'in other words'."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Maybe my friend is at home now.", "ko": "어쩌면 친구가 지금 집에 있을지도 몰라요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "It looks like the bus will probably be late.", "ko": "아무래도 버스가 늦을 것 같아요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "My grandfather is eighty years old. (respectful)", "ko": "할아버지는 연세가 여든이세요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "My friend came out in exercise clothes.", "ko": "친구가 운동복 차림으로 나왔어요."}], "reading": {"ko": "이번 주말에 할머니를 뵈러 대구에 가요. 할머니는 연세가 많으시지만 아직 건강하세요. 할머니는 집 앞에서 채소와 꽃을 가꾸세요. 지난번에 저는 운동복 차림으로 할머니 집에 갔어요. 그래서 이번에는 예쁜 옷을 입을 거예요. 어쩌면 오빠도 같이 갈지도 몰라요. 아무래도 이번 주말은 아주 바쁠 것 같아요.", "en": "This weekend I'm going to Daegu to see my grandmother. My grandmother is elderly, but she is still healthy. She grows vegetables and flowers in front of her house. Last time I went to her house in exercise clothes. So this time I'm going to wear nice clothes. Maybe my older brother will come with me too. This weekend will probably be very busy.", "questions": [{"q": "할머니는 집 앞에서 뭘 가꾸세요?", "a": "채소와 꽃을 가꾸세요."}, {"q": "지난번에 이 사람은 어떤 옷차림으로 갔어요?", "a": "운동복 차림으로 갔어요."}, {"q": "누가 같이 갈지도 몰라요?", "a": "오빠가 같이 갈지도 몰라요."}]}}}, {"n": 60, "title": "Lesson 60: Difficult words: 차다, 가득, 왠지, 설마, 찍다, 다행, ~달", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-3-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-3-lessons-59-66/lesson-60/", "vocab": [{"ko": "차다", "en": "to be full", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "가득", "en": "filled (adverb)", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "자신감", "en": "confidence", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "표정", "en": "facial expression", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "왠지", "en": "for some reason", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "설마", "en": "don't tell me...", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "찍다", "en": "to take (a photo); to stamp; to dip", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "도장", "en": "personal seal, stamp", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "다행하다", "en": "to be fortunate", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "~달", "en": "sometime in (a month)", "pos": "other"}], "grammar": [{"point": "(가득) 차다 / 차 있다 (to be full)", "explanation": "차다 means 'to become full'. The thing that gets full is the subject, and what fills it is marked with (으)로: 버스가 사람들로 찼어요. You can also mark the place with 에 and make the filler the subject: 병에 물이 찼어요. Either way there is no object.\n\n차다 describes the moment of filling up, so to say something is full now, Koreans use the past tense: 찼어요 = 'it's full' (it filled up and still is). 차 있다 says the same thing as an ongoing state. The stem 차 ends in ㅏ, so 차 + 았어요 → 찼어요 and 차 + 아 있어요 → 차 있어요.\n\n가득 ('completely, to the brim') often comes right before it: 가득 찼어요. To describe a noun, use the past modifier 찬: 사람들로 가득 찬 버스 (a bus packed with people). With feelings, Noun에 찬 means 'full of': 자신감에 찬 표정 (an expression full of confidence). Don't confuse it with the unrelated 차다 meaning 'to kick' (공을 차다), which does take an object.", "examples": [{"ko": "버스가 사람들로 가득 찼어요.", "en": "The bus is packed with people."}, {"ko": "병에 물이 가득 차 있어요.", "en": "The bottle is full of water."}, {"ko": "그는 자신감에 찬 표정으로 말했어요.", "en": "He spoke with a confident expression."}, {"ko": "주차장이 차로 가득 찼어요.", "en": "The parking lot is full of cars.", "note": "차 (car) and 차다 (to be full) are unrelated words."}, {"ko": "냉장고에 음식이 가득 차 있어요.", "en": "The fridge is full of food."}, {"ko": "사람들로 가득 찬 식당에 들어갔어요.", "en": "We went into a restaurant packed with people."}]}, {"point": "왠지 (for some reason)", "explanation": "왠지 means 'for some reason' or 'somehow, I don't know why'. It is a shortened form of 왜 그런지 ('why it is so'). Use it when you feel something, or something seems a certain way, but you can't point to a clear cause. It's very common with moods, hunches and first impressions.\n\n왠지 usually comes at the start of the sentence, and the rest of the sentence is normal: 왠지 + 기분이 좋아요. It often goes with feeling words and guesses, such as 좋다, 싫다, ~고 싶다 and ~ㄹ/을 것 같다.\n\nSpelling tip: 왠지 is the only common word spelled with 왠. Other words use 웬, as in 웬일이에요? ('What brings you here?'). Also, 왠지 doesn't ask a question; to ask 'why', use 왜.", "examples": [{"ko": "오늘은 왠지 기분이 좋아요.", "en": "For some reason I'm in a good mood today."}, {"ko": "왠지 그 사람이 마음에 안 들어요.", "en": "I don't know why, but I don't like that person."}, {"ko": "왠지 집에 일찍 가고 싶어요.", "en": "For some reason I want to go home early."}, {"ko": "왠지 오늘 좋은 일이 있을 것 같아요.", "en": "For some reason I feel like something good will happen today."}, {"ko": "왠지 그 노래를 들으면 슬퍼져요.", "en": "For some reason that song makes me sad when I listen to it.", "note": "슬프다 drops ㅡ: 슬프 + 어지다 → 슬퍼지다."}, {"ko": "왠지 이 길은 처음이 아닌 것 같아요.", "en": "Somehow it feels like this isn't my first time on this road."}]}, {"point": "설마 (don't tell me...)", "explanation": "설마 shows disbelief. The speaker suspects something might be true but can hardly believe it, or really hopes it isn't so. In English: 'Don't tell me...', 'Surely not...?', 'You didn't..., did you?'.\n\nPut 설마 at the start of an ordinary question; nothing else changes: 혼자 갔어요? → 설마 혼자 갔어요? It's most often used with the past tense, about something that may already have happened, but the present works too: 설마 오늘이 시험이에요? Say it with surprised, rising intonation.\n\n설마 can also be a reply on its own: 설마요! ('No way!'). Compare with 왠지: 왠지 = 'I have a feeling, I don't know why'; 설마 = 'I can hardly believe this could be true'.", "examples": [{"ko": "설마 벌써 다 먹었어요?", "en": "Don't tell me you already ate it all?"}, {"ko": "설마 혼자 갔어요?", "en": "Don't tell me you went alone?"}, {"ko": "설마 그 사람이 거짓말을 했어요?", "en": "Surely he didn't lie?"}, {"ko": "설마 오늘이 시험이에요?", "en": "Don't tell me the exam is today?"}, {"ko": "설마 아직도 안 일어났어요?", "en": "Don't tell me you're still not up?"}, {"ko": "설마요! 정말이에요?", "en": "No way! Really?"}]}, {"point": "찍다 (to touch one thing onto another)", "explanation": "찍다's core meaning is pressing one thing onto another so it makes contact or leaves a mark. The English translation changes with the object, but the picture stays the same. 사진을 찍다 is 'to take a photo', and 영화나 동영상을 찍다 is 'to film'. 도장을 찍다 is 'to stamp a seal'. Food + 에 찍다 is 'to dip food in a sauce'. Casually, 답을 찍다 means 'to guess an answer' on a test, as if jabbing at a choice.\n\nConjugation: the stem 찍 ends in a consonant and its vowel is ㅣ, so it takes 어요: 찍 + 어요 → 찍어요; past 찍었어요; future 찍을 거예요.\n\nTwo very common combinations: ask someone to take a photo with ~아/어 주다 (사진 좀 찍어 주세요), and say 'dip and eat' with ~아/어서 (간장에 찍어서 먹어요).", "examples": [{"ko": "친구가 제 사진을 많이 찍었어요.", "en": "My friend took a lot of pictures of me."}, {"ko": "여기에 도장을 찍어 주세요.", "en": "Please stamp your seal here."}, {"ko": "만두를 간장에 찍어서 먹어요.", "en": "I dip the dumplings in soy sauce and eat them."}, {"ko": "여기서 사진을 찍어도 돼요?", "en": "Is it okay to take photos here?"}, {"ko": "답을 몰라서 그냥 찍었어요.", "en": "I didn't know the answer, so I just guessed.", "note": "Casual use: 찍다 here means picking an answer at random."}, {"ko": "이 영화는 부산에서 찍었어요.", "en": "This movie was filmed in Busan."}]}, {"point": "~아/어서 다행이다 / 다행히 (thankfully)", "explanation": "다행 means 'good fortune' or 'relief'. 다행이다 ('it's a relief') is how you say 'Thank goodness...' or 'I'm glad that...' when something could have gone badly but didn't.\n\nTo say what you're relieved about, put that clause in ~아/어서 ('because') and finish with 다행이에요. Use the normal ~아/어서 rules: ㅏ/ㅗ stems take 아서 (찾다 → 찾아서), others take 어서 (없다 → 없어서), 하다 becomes 해서. Example: 길이 안 막히다 → 안 막혀서 → 안 막혀서 다행이에요. Even when the event is in the past, the ~아/어서 part stays in the present form (다쳤어서 is wrong; use 다쳐서). The tense goes on 다행이다: 다행이었어요 = 'it was a relief'.\n\n다행히 is the adverb: 'fortunately, luckily'. Put it at the start of an ordinary sentence: 다행히 시험에 늦지 않았어요. On its own, 정말 다행이에요! is a common reaction: 'What a relief!'", "examples": [{"ko": "길이 안 막혀서 다행이에요.", "en": "Thankfully the roads weren't jammed."}, {"ko": "아무도 다치지 않아서 다행이에요.", "en": "Thank goodness nobody got hurt."}, {"ko": "다행히 시험에 늦지 않았어요.", "en": "Fortunately I wasn't late for the exam."}, {"ko": "지갑을 찾아서 정말 다행이에요.", "en": "Thank goodness I found my wallet."}, {"ko": "다행히 버스가 아직 안 떠났어요.", "en": "Luckily the bus hadn't left yet."}, {"ko": "날씨가 좋아서 다행이었어요.", "en": "It was a relief that the weather was good."}]}, {"point": "Month + 달 (sometime in ...)", "explanation": "Adding 달 after a month name, as in 3월 달, means 'in March', with the feeling of 'sometime during that month' when the exact day isn't known or doesn't matter. It's casual and spoken. In writing and careful speech, the month alone (3월에) is standard, and some people find 3월 달 redundant, since 월 and 달 both mean 'month'.\n\nUse it like any time word: add 에 for 'in': 3월 달 + 에 → 3월 달에. Read months with Sino-Korean numbers as usual, with two special cases: 6월 is 유월 and 10월 is 시월.\n\nThis 달 is the same word you use in 이번 달 (this month), 다음 달 (next month) and 지난달 (last month).", "examples": [{"ko": "저는 3월 달에 이사할 거예요.", "en": "I'm moving sometime in March."}, {"ko": "언니는 10월 달에 결혼해요.", "en": "My sister is getting married sometime in October."}, {"ko": "5월 달에 한국에 가요.", "en": "I'm going to Korea sometime in May."}, {"ko": "6월 달에는 비가 많이 와요.", "en": "It rains a lot in June.", "note": "6월 is read 유월, not 육월."}, {"ko": "10월 달에 시간이 있어요?", "en": "Do you have time sometime in October?", "note": "10월 is read 시월, not 십월."}, {"ko": "작년 12월 달에 처음 한국에 갔어요.", "en": "I went to Korea for the first time last December."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "지하철이 사람들___ 가득 찼어요.", "answer": "로", "options": ["로", "으로", "를", "에서"], "en": "The subway is packed with people.", "why": "What fills a space takes (으)로, and a noun ending in ㄹ takes 로."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "컵에 물이 가득 ___ 있어요.", "answer": "차", "options": ["차", "찬", "찼", "차는"], "en": "The cup is full of water.", "why": "차다 + 아 있다 becomes 차 있다, a state of being full."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "오늘은 ___ 기분이 안 좋아요.", "answer": "왠지", "options": ["왠지", "웬지", "왜", "설마"], "en": "For some reason I'm not in a good mood today.", "why": "왠지 (from 왜 그런지) means 'for some reason'; 웬지 is a common misspelling."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "___ 벌써 숙제를 다 했어요?", "answer": "설마", "options": ["설마", "왠지", "다행히", "즉"], "en": "Don't tell me you already finished all your homework?", "why": "설마 at the start of a question shows you can hardly believe it."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "이 종이에 도장을 ___ 주세요.", "answer": "찍어", "options": ["찍어", "찍", "찍고", "찍는"], "en": "Please stamp your seal on this paper.", "why": "찍다 takes its 아/어 form before 주세요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "비가 오기 전에 집에 도착___ 다행이에요.", "answer": "해서", "options": ["해서", "하고", "하면", "하지만"], "en": "Thankfully I got home before it rained.", "why": "~아/어서 다행이다 expresses relief that something happened."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "___ 지갑을 찾았어요.", "answer": "다행히", "options": ["다행히", "다행이", "다행해", "다행을"], "en": "Fortunately I found my wallet.", "why": "The adverb form is 다행히, 'fortunately'."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "친구는 12월 ___ 한국에 와요.", "answer": "달에", "options": ["달에", "달을", "달이", "달로"], "en": "My friend is coming to Korea sometime in December.", "why": "Month + 달에 means 'sometime in that month', and time takes 에."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "The bag is full of books.", "ko": "가방이 책으로 가득 찼어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "For some reason I want to eat Korean food today.", "ko": "오늘은 왠지 한국 음식을 먹고 싶어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Thankfully it didn't rain.", "ko": "비가 안 와서 다행이에요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Could you take a picture of me?", "ko": "제 사진을 찍어 줄래요?"}], "reading": {"ko": "토요일에 친구와 같이 공원에 갔어요. 공원은 사람들로 가득 찼어요. 우리는 꽃 앞에서 사진을 많이 찍었어요. 그런데 집에 왔을 때 카메라가 없었어요. 저는 친구에게 전화했어요. 다행히 친구가 제 카메라를 가지고 있었어요. 카메라를 찾아서 정말 다행이에요. 우리는 5월 달에 다시 공원에 갈 거예요.", "en": "On Saturday I went to the park with a friend. The park was packed with people. We took lots of photos in front of the flowers. But when I got home, I didn't have my camera. I called my friend. Fortunately my friend had my camera. I'm really relieved I found it. We're going to the park again sometime in May.", "questions": [{"q": "공원에 사람이 많았어요?", "a": "네, 사람들로 가득 찼어요."}, {"q": "집에 왔을 때 뭐가 없었어요?", "a": "카메라가 없었어요."}, {"q": "카메라는 누가 가지고 있었어요?", "a": "친구가 가지고 있었어요."}]}}}, {"n": 61, "title": "Lesson 61: To wish/to hope: 바라다, ~았/었으면 좋겠다", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-3-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-3-lessons-59-66/lesson-61/", "vocab": [{"ko": "쌍둥이", "en": "twin", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "보충", "en": "supplement", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "백조", "en": "swan", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "실내", "en": "interior", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "정책", "en": "policy", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "통화", "en": "currency", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "단백질", "en": "protein", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "지방", "en": "fat", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "탄수화물", "en": "carbohydrate", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "양", "en": "amount", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "야간", "en": "night-time (shifts, classes)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "최선", "en": "one’s best", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "사전", "en": "beforehand, prior", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "쌍", "en": "a pair", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "의도적", "en": "intentional", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "헤엄치다", "en": "to swim, to move through water", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "해고하다", "en": "to fire (from a job)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "건네다", "en": "to hand-over, to pass on", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "해설하다", "en": "to explain (commentary)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "다하다", "en": "to do all of, use up", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "데리러 오다", "en": "to come pick someone up", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "데리러 가다", "en": "to go pick someone up", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "추가하다", "en": "to add to, to supplement", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "의도하다", "en": "to intend", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "청결하다", "en": "to be clean (hygienic)", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "꾸준하다", "en": "to be steady, consistent", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "한가롭다", "en": "to be leisurely", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "딱", "en": "exactly, perfectly, precisely", "pos": "adverb"}, {"ko": "무조건", "en": "unconditionally", "pos": "adverb"}], "grammar": [{"point": "~기(를) 바라다 (to hope / request)", "explanation": "바라다 means 'to hope' or 'to wish for'. You can hope for a noun directly (행복을 바라요, 'I wish for happiness'), but to hope that something happens you need a whole clause. Turn the verb into a noun with ~기, add the object particle 를, then 바라다: 건강하다 → 건강하기 → 건강하기를 바랍니다. The 를 is often dropped: 건강하기 바랍니다.\n\n~기 attaches the same way to every stem, vowel or consonant: 오다 → 오기, 있다 → 있기, 낫다 → 낫기. When the hope is about someone you respect, put 시 before 기: 건강하 + 시 + 기 → 건강하시기를 바랍니다.\n\n바랍니다 is formal, so it's common in speeches, cards, emails and announcements. In announcements, ~기 바랍니다 tells people what to do in an official way: 조용히 해 주시기 바랍니다 ('please be quiet'). It sounds stiff, so don't use it to ask a friend for something.\n\nSpelling: the polite form is 바라요 (바라 + 아요 → 바라요). Many people say and even write 바래요, but 바라요 is standard.", "examples": [{"ko": "모두 건강하시기를 바랍니다.", "en": "I hope everyone stays healthy."}, {"ko": "좋은 결과가 있기를 바랍니다.", "en": "I hope there will be a good result."}, {"ko": "실내에서는 조용히 해 주시기 바랍니다.", "en": "Please be quiet indoors."}, {"ko": "즐거운 시간 보내시기 바랍니다.", "en": "I hope you have a good time."}, {"ko": "동생이 빨리 낫기를 바라요.", "en": "I hope my younger sibling gets better soon.", "note": "낫다 is ㅅ irregular, but ~기 starts with a consonant, so it stays 낫기."}, {"ko": "저는 큰 것을 바라지 않아요.", "en": "I'm not hoping for anything big."}]}, {"point": "~았/었으면 좋겠다 (I hope / I wish)", "explanation": "~았/었으면 좋겠다 literally means 'if (it) happened, it would be good'. Use it to say what you hope or wish for: something in the future (날씨가 맑았으면 좋겠어요) or something that isn't true now (돈이 많았으면 좋겠어요). Even though there's a past marker inside, it isn't about the past. Treat it as one fixed unit meaning 'I hope / I wish'.\n\nTo form it, take the past-tense stem and add 으면 좋겠다. 맑다 → 맑았 + 으면 → 맑았으면 좋겠어요. 끝나다 → 끝났 + 으면 → 끝났으면 좋겠어요. 오다 → 왔으면; 하다 → 했으면. With a noun, use 이다: 주말이었으면 / 휴가였으면 좋겠어요. For a negative wish, put 안 in front: 비가 안 왔으면 좋겠어요.\n\n~(으)면 좋겠다 without the past marker means nearly the same (비가 오면 좋겠어요). The past version sounds a little more wishful and is very common in speech. Compared with ~기(를) 바라다, this is the everyday, conversational way to say 'I hope'; ~기 바랍니다 sounds formal.", "examples": [{"ko": "내일 날씨가 맑았으면 좋겠어요.", "en": "I hope the weather is clear tomorrow."}, {"ko": "친구가 저를 데리러 왔으면 좋겠어요.", "en": "I wish my friend would come pick me up."}, {"ko": "시험이 빨리 끝났으면 좋겠어요.", "en": "I wish the exam would end quickly."}, {"ko": "돈이 많았으면 좋겠어요.", "en": "I wish I had a lot of money."}, {"ko": "제가 키가 더 컸으면 좋겠어요.", "en": "I wish I were taller.", "note": "크다 drops ㅡ: 크 + 었 → 컸."}, {"ko": "오늘이 주말이었으면 좋겠어요.", "en": "I wish today were the weekend."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "모두 행복하___ 바랍니다.", "answer": "시기를", "options": ["시기를", "시는", "셔서", "시고"], "en": "I hope you are all happy.", "why": "바라다 takes a clause nominalised with ~기(를); 시 adds respect."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "좋은 일만 ___ 바랍니다.", "answer": "있기를", "options": ["있기를", "있는", "있어서", "있으면"], "en": "I hope only good things happen.", "why": "Before 바라다, the verb becomes a noun with ~기를."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "수업 전에 휴대폰을 꺼 주시기 ___.", "answer": "바랍니다", "options": ["바랍니다", "바랍니까", "바라기", "바랬습니다"], "en": "Please turn off your phones before class. (formal announcement)", "why": "~아/어 주시기 바랍니다 is a formal request in announcements."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "내일 날씨가 ___ 좋겠어요.", "answer": "좋았으면", "options": ["좋았으면", "좋았고", "좋았어서", "좋기를"], "en": "I hope the weather is nice tomorrow.", "why": "~았/었으면 좋겠다 is a fixed unit for wishes, even about the future."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "방학이 빨리 ___ 좋겠어요.", "answer": "왔으면", "options": ["왔으면", "오았으면", "와으면", "왔면"], "en": "I wish vacation would come soon.", "why": "오 + 았으면 contracts to 왔으면."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "시간이 조금 더 ___ 좋겠어요.", "answer": "있었으면", "options": ["있었으면", "있으었면", "있어으면", "있었면"], "en": "I wish I had a little more time.", "why": "있다 takes 었으면 because its last vowel is not ㅏ or ㅗ."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "아빠가 역으로 저를 ___ 왔으면 좋겠어요.", "answer": "데리러", "options": ["데리러", "데리려", "데리면", "데리는"], "en": "I wish Dad would come pick me up at the station.", "why": "데리러 오다 means 'to come pick someone up'."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "모든 일이 잘되기___ 바라요.", "answer": "를", "options": ["를", "을", "가", "에"], "en": "I hope everything goes well.", "why": "~기 ends in a vowel, so the object marker is 를."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I hope it doesn't rain tomorrow.", "ko": "내일 비가 안 왔으면 좋겠어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Grandmother, I hope you stay healthy. (formal)", "ko": "할머니, 건강하시기를 바랍니다."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I wish I were a twin.", "ko": "제가 쌍둥이였으면 좋겠어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Please do your best. (formal request)", "ko": "최선을 다해 주시기 바랍니다."}], "reading": {"ko": "제 동생들은 쌍둥이예요. 다음 주에 동생들이 큰 시험을 봐요. 동생들은 요즘 밤까지 열심히 공부해요. 저는 동생들이 최선을 다했으면 좋겠어요. 시험이 끝나면 제가 학교로 동생들을 데리러 갈 거예요. 그리고 같이 맛있는 음식을 먹었으면 좋겠어요. 동생들에게 좋은 결과가 있기를 바라요.", "en": "My younger siblings are twins. Next week they have a big exam. These days they study hard until night. I hope they do their best. When the exam is over, I'll go to the school to pick them up. And I hope we can eat delicious food together. I hope they get good results.", "questions": [{"q": "동생들은 다음 주에 뭘 해요?", "a": "큰 시험을 봐요."}, {"q": "시험이 끝나면 이 사람은 뭘 할 거예요?", "a": "학교로 동생들을 데리러 갈 거예요."}, {"q": "이 사람은 동생들에게 뭐가 있기를 바라요?", "a": "좋은 결과가 있기를 바라요."}]}}}, {"n": 62, "title": "Lesson 62: While: ~(으)면서, ~(으)며", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-3-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-3-lessons-59-66/lesson-62/", "vocab": [{"ko": "일정", "en": "schedule (itinerary)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "앞문", "en": "front door", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "뒷문", "en": "back door", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "기념품", "en": "souvenir", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "기념비", "en": "monument", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "학업", "en": "studies", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "사안", "en": "issue/matter", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "무단", "en": "without permission", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "상반기", "en": "the first half of the year", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "하반기", "en": "the second half of the year", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "사계절", "en": "four seasons", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "관광객", "en": "tourists", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "영상", "en": "picture, image", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "동영상", "en": "video", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "갈다", "en": "to change/replace; to grind/sharpen", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "꼬집다", "en": "to pinch", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "쌓다", "en": "to build up, to stack up", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "기념하다", "en": "to commemorate", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "뒤따르다", "en": "to follow, to go after", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "진행하다", "en": "to progress", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "누르다", "en": "to press", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "자라다", "en": "to grow up", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "횡단하다", "en": "to cross (formal: traverse)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "판매하다", "en": "to sell (formal/commercial)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "쌓이다", "en": "to be stacked/build up", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "흐리다", "en": "to be cloudy, hazy", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "적발 시", "en": "if caught", "pos": "other"}, {"ko": "문득", "en": "suddenly (a thought strikes)", "pos": "adverb"}], "grammar": [{"point": "~(으)면서 (while doing two things at once)", "explanation": "~(으)면서 means 'while': the same person does two things at the same time. The action before ~(으)면서 is the background; the verb at the end is the main action. Tense goes only on the final verb: 마시면서 ... 읽었어요 is past because 읽었어요 is past.\n\nForming it: vowel stem + 면서 (마시다 → 마시면서; 보다 → 보면서). Consonant stem + 으면서 (먹다 → 먹 + 으면서 → 먹으면서). ㄹ stems add 면서 directly (놀다 → 놀면서; 살다 → 살면서). Irregulars: ㄷ verbs change ㄷ to ㄹ (듣다 → 들으면서; 걷다 → 걸으면서), and ㅂ verbs change ㅂ to 우 (돕다 → 도우면서).\n\nBoth actions must have the same subject. If two different people act at the same time, use ~는 동안 instead. With posture verbs like 앉다 or 서다, use ~아/어서 (앉아서 공부해요), because 앉으면서 would mean you studied while in the act of sitting down. With 자라다, ~(으)면서 gives 'grew up doing ...': 한국 음식을 먹으면서 자랐어요.", "examples": [{"ko": "음악을 들으면서 공부해요.", "en": "I study while listening to music."}, {"ko": "커피를 마시면서 신문을 읽었어요.", "en": "I read the newspaper while drinking coffee."}, {"ko": "저는 한국 음식을 먹으면서 자랐어요.", "en": "I grew up eating Korean food."}, {"ko": "길을 걸으면서 전화했어요.", "en": "I talked on the phone while walking down the street.", "note": "걷다 is ㄷ irregular: 걷 → 걸 + 으면서 → 걸으면서."}, {"ko": "아이들은 놀면서 배워요.", "en": "Children learn while playing."}, {"ko": "텔레비전을 보면서 밥을 먹지 마세요.", "en": "Don't eat while watching TV."}]}, {"point": "~(으)면서 with adjectives and 이다", "explanation": "With adjectives, ~(으)면서 joins two qualities that something has at the same time: 싸면서 맛있어요 ('cheap and tasty at the same time'). With 이다, it says one person or thing has two roles: 선생님이면서 작가예요 ('a teacher and also a writer').\n\nThe forming rule is the same as with verbs. Vowel stem + 면서: 싸다 → 싸면서; 흐리다 → 흐리면서. Consonant stem + 으면서: 넓다 → 넓으면서. ㅂ adjectives change ㅂ to 우: 가볍다 → 가벼우면서. With a noun, attach 이다: 선생님 + 이면서 → 선생님이면서; 가수 + 이면서 → 가수이면서 (in speech often shortened to 가수면서).\n\nIt's close to ~고 (싸고 맛있어요), but ~고 simply lists. ~(으)면서 stresses that both qualities exist together, 'both ... and ...', often a pleasant or surprising combination. When the two qualities clash, it moves toward the 'even though' meaning in the next point.", "examples": [{"ko": "이 식당은 싸면서 맛있어요.", "en": "This restaurant is cheap and tasty at the same time."}, {"ko": "그는 선생님이면서 작가예요.", "en": "He is a teacher and also a writer."}, {"ko": "오늘은 흐리면서 추워요.", "en": "Today it's cloudy and cold."}, {"ko": "이 가방은 가벼우면서 튼튼해요.", "en": "This bag is light and sturdy at the same time.", "note": "가볍다 is ㅂ irregular: 가볍 → 가벼우 + 면서."}, {"ko": "제 친구는 가수이면서 배우예요.", "en": "My friend is a singer and also an actor."}, {"ko": "그 방은 넓으면서 밝아요.", "en": "That room is spacious and bright."}]}, {"point": "~(으)면서(도) for contradiction", "explanation": "Sometimes the two things joined by ~(으)면서 don't fit together, and the speaker finds that strange, unfair or annoying. Then it means 'even though' or 'yet': 다 알면서 왜 물어봐요? = 'You know it all, yet you're asking?'. It's common when complaining or pointing out that someone is being inconsistent.\n\nThe form is the same as before: vowel or ㄹ stem + 면서 (알다 → 알면서), consonant stem + 으면서 (없다 → 없으면서). Adding 도 (~(으)면서도) makes the clash stronger and clearer. Without 도, context tells you whether it means 'while' or 'even though'. As usual, the tense goes on the final verb: 알면서도 말 안 했어요 ('I knew, yet I didn't say').\n\nCompare with ~지만 ('but'). ~지만 simply contrasts two facts. ~(으)면서(도) is about one subject who has or does two conflicting things at once, often with a judging tone.", "examples": [{"ko": "그는 바쁘다고 하면서 하루 종일 텔레비전을 봐요.", "en": "He says he's busy, yet he watches TV all day."}, {"ko": "다 알면서 왜 물어봐요?", "en": "You know everything, so why are you asking?"}, {"ko": "돈이 없으면서도 비싼 옷을 샀어요.", "en": "She bought expensive clothes even though she has no money."}, {"ko": "저는 알면서도 아무 말도 안 했어요.", "en": "Even though I knew, I didn't say anything."}, {"ko": "그는 배가 부르면서도 계속 먹어요.", "en": "He keeps eating even though he's full."}, {"ko": "피곤하면서 왜 안 자요?", "en": "You're tired, so why aren't you sleeping?"}]}, {"point": "~(으)며 (formal 'while' / 'and')", "explanation": "~(으)며 is a shorter, formal relative of ~(으)면서. It attaches the same way: vowel or ㄹ stem + 며 (깨끗하다 → 깨끗하며; 살다 → 살며), consonant stem + 으며 (찍다 → 찍으며; 많다 → 많으며). ㅂ irregulars use 우 (아름답다 → 아름다우며), and ㄷ irregulars use ㄹ (듣다 → 들으며).\n\nIts main job is like ~고: listing facts or linking related clauses ('and'). You'll see it in news, reports, signs, essays and speeches, usually with ~습니다 or written endings. When listing, each clause can carry its own past tense: 늘다 → 늘었 + 으며 → 늘었으며.\n\nIt can also mean 'while', like ~(으)면서: 사진을 찍으며 웃었습니다. That's fine in writing, but in everyday conversation ~(으)면서 is far more natural. Tip: when reading, try ~(으)며 as ~고 first; if that doesn't fit, read it as 'while'. When speaking, use ~고 and ~(으)면서 instead.", "examples": [{"ko": "이 도시는 깨끗하며 조용합니다.", "en": "This city is clean and quiet."}, {"ko": "관광객들이 사진을 찍으며 웃었습니다.", "en": "The tourists laughed while taking pictures."}, {"ko": "상반기에는 판매가 늘었으며 하반기에도 늘 것입니다.", "en": "Sales rose in the first half and will also rise in the second half."}, {"ko": "이 섬은 사계절이 아름다우며 관광객이 많습니다.", "en": "This island is beautiful in all four seasons, and there are many tourists.", "note": "아름답다 is ㅂ irregular: 아름답 → 아름다우 + 며."}, {"ko": "제 언니는 회사원이며 두 아이의 엄마입니다.", "en": "My older sister is an office worker and the mother of two children."}, {"ko": "그는 음악을 들으며 산책했습니다.", "en": "He took a walk while listening to music."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "저는 음악을 ___ 운동해요.", "answer": "들으면서", "options": ["들으면서", "듣으면서", "들면서", "듣면서"], "en": "I exercise while listening to music.", "why": "듣다 is ㄷ irregular, so it becomes 들으면서."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "친구와 이야기를 ___ 걸었어요.", "answer": "하면서", "options": ["하면서", "하으면서", "해면서", "하며서"], "en": "I walked while talking with my friend.", "why": "A vowel stem takes ~면서 directly."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "그는 서울에 ___ 한국어를 배웠어요.", "answer": "살면서", "options": ["살면서", "살으면서", "사면서", "살며서"], "en": "He learned Korean while living in Seoul.", "why": "An ㄹ stem takes ~면서 directly, with no 으."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "의자에 ___ 책을 읽었어요.", "answer": "앉아서", "options": ["앉아서", "앉으면서", "앉면서", "앉어서"], "en": "I sat on a chair and read a book.", "why": "Posture verbs use ~아/어서; 앉으면서 would mean 'while sitting down'."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "이 가방은 ___ 가벼워요.", "answer": "싸면서", "options": ["싸면서", "싸으면서", "싼면서", "싸서면서"], "en": "This bag is cheap and light at the same time.", "why": "With adjectives, ~(으)면서 joins two qualities at once."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "제 친구는 학생___ 회사원이에요.", "answer": "이면서", "options": ["이면서", "면서", "으면서", "이며서"], "en": "My friend is a student and an office worker at the same time.", "why": "After a consonant, 이다 becomes 이면서."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "그는 배가 ___ 또 라면을 먹었어요.", "answer": "부르면서도", "options": ["부르면서도", "불러면서도", "부르으면서도", "불르면서도"], "en": "He was full, yet he ate ramen again.", "why": "~(으)면서도 links two clashing facts: 'even though'."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "이 공원은 넓___ 조용합니다.", "answer": "으며", "options": ["으며", "며", "이며", "어며"], "en": "This park is spacious and quiet. (formal writing)", "why": "~(으)며 links clauses in formal writing, and a consonant stem takes 으며."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I read a book while drinking tea.", "ko": "차를 마시면서 책을 읽었어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I grew up watching Korean dramas.", "ko": "저는 한국 드라마를 보면서 자랐어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Today it's cloudy and windy.", "ko": "오늘은 흐리면서 바람이 많이 불어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "He says he has no money, yet he buys souvenirs every day.", "ko": "그는 돈이 없다고 하면서 매일 기념품을 사요."}], "reading": {"ko": "저는 지난달에 부산을 여행했어요. 부산은 바다가 아름다우면서 음식도 맛있어요. 저는 바다를 보면서 커피를 마셨어요. 관광객들이 바다 앞에서 사진을 찍으며 웃고 있었어요. 저도 동영상을 많이 찍었어요. 그리고 친구들에게 줄 기념품을 샀어요. 일정이 바빴지만 정말 즐거웠어요.", "en": "Last month I traveled to Busan. Busan has a beautiful sea and tasty food too. I drank coffee while looking at the sea. Tourists were laughing while taking pictures in front of the sea. I took lots of videos too. And I bought souvenirs to give to my friends. The schedule was busy, but it was really fun.", "questions": [{"q": "부산은 어때요?", "a": "바다가 아름다우면서 음식도 맛있어요."}, {"q": "이 사람은 바다를 보면서 뭘 했어요?", "a": "커피를 마셨어요."}, {"q": "이 사람은 친구들에게 뭘 줄 거예요?", "a": "기념품을 줄 거예요."}]}}}, {"n": 63, "title": "Lesson 63: ~ㄹ/을까(요), ~ㄹ/을게요", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-3-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-3-lessons-59-66/lesson-63/", "vocab": [{"ko": "책자", "en": "booklet, pamphlet", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "바퀴", "en": "wheel", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "기운", "en": "energy, vigor", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "무리", "en": "group (crowd, herd)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "정체", "en": "identity", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "교원", "en": "teacher (official: teaching staff)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "살인", "en": "murder", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "살인자", "en": "murderer", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "선착순", "en": "first come first served", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "초과하다", "en": "to exceed", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "울리다", "en": "to make somebody cry", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "결재하다", "en": "to approve", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "퇴근하다", "en": "to leave work", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "흘러나오다", "en": "to trickle out of", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "주고받다", "en": "to exchange (give and receive)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "고용하다", "en": "to hire", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "공고하다", "en": "to announce (post an official notice)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "연기하다", "en": "to act (perform a role)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "이루다", "en": "to achieve, accomplish", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "이루어지다", "en": "to be made up of; to come true", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "부서지다", "en": "to be smashed into pieces", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "부러지다", "en": "to be broken in half", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "위태롭다", "en": "to be risky, critical", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "부유하다", "en": "to be rich", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "번거롭다", "en": "to be cumbersome", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "모금", "en": "sip/breath (counter)", "pos": "other"}], "grammar": [{"point": "~ㄹ/을까? (wondering to oneself)", "explanation": "~ㄹ/을까 at the end of a sentence (without 요) is a question you ask yourself, or think out loud. With your own action it means 'Should I...?' (이 책을 살까?). With something you can't control, like weather or other people, it means 'I wonder if...' (내일 비가 올까?).\n\nForming it: vowel stem + ㄹ까 (사다 → 사 + ㄹ까 → 살까; 오다 → 올까). Consonant stem + 을까 (먹다 → 먹을까; 입다 → 입을까). ㄹ stems just add 까 (놀다 → 놀까; 만들다 → 만들까). Irregulars: 듣다 → 들을까, 춥다 → 추울까. To wonder about the past, use ~았/었을까: 벌써 끝났을까? ('I wonder if it's already over').\n\nWithout 요 it's casual. Said to yourself it's self-talk, but said to a close friend the same sentence can be a real question or a suggestion ('Shall we...?'). Context and tone tell you which. The following points cover the polite 요 versions.", "examples": [{"ko": "오늘 저녁에 뭘 먹을까?", "en": "What should I eat tonight?"}, {"ko": "이 책을 살까?", "en": "Should I buy this book?"}, {"ko": "내일 비가 올까?", "en": "I wonder if it'll rain tomorrow."}, {"ko": "오늘 무슨 옷을 입을까?", "en": "What should I wear today?"}, {"ko": "주말에 뭐 하고 놀까?", "en": "What should I do for fun this weekend?"}, {"ko": "밖에 많이 추울까?", "en": "I wonder if it's really cold outside.", "note": "춥다 is ㅂ irregular: 춥 → 추우 + ㄹ까 → 추울까."}]}, {"point": "~ㄹ/을까요? (shall we...?)", "explanation": "When you and the listener would do something together, ~ㄹ/을까요? means 'Shall we...?'. It's a soft suggestion that also asks for the other person's opinion, so it sounds polite and considerate. Add 우리 or 같이 to make 'we' clear, and use question words for 'where/when/what shall we...?': 어디에서 만날까요?\n\nForming it: vowel stem + ㄹ까요 (보다 → 볼까요; 쉬다 → 쉴까요). Consonant stem + 을까요 (먹다 → 먹을까요; 앉다 → 앉을까요). ㄹ stems add 까요 (만들다 → 만들까요). ㄷ irregulars: 걷다 → 걸을까요.\n\nTo agree, say 좋아요, 그래요, or 그럴까요? Compare with lesson 44: ~ㅂ/읍시다 ('let's') is more direct and can sound bossy toward elders, and ~ㄹ/을래요? asks what the listener wants to do. ~ㄹ/을까요? is the safest way to suggest something politely.", "examples": [{"ko": "같이 영화 볼까요?", "en": "Shall we watch a movie together?"}, {"ko": "우리 어디에서 만날까요?", "en": "Where shall we meet?"}, {"ko": "잠깐 쉴까요?", "en": "Shall we take a short break?"}, {"ko": "오늘 점심은 뭘 먹을까요?", "en": "What shall we have for lunch today?"}, {"ko": "공원에서 조금 걸을까요?", "en": "Shall we walk a bit in the park?", "note": "걷다 is ㄷ irregular: 걷 → 걸 + 을까요."}, {"ko": "내일 몇 시에 출발할까요?", "en": "What time shall we leave tomorrow?"}]}, {"point": "~ㄹ/을까요? (shall I...?)", "explanation": "When only the speaker would act, ~ㄹ/을까요? offers to do something and checks whether the listener wants it: 'Shall I...?' or 'Do you want me to...?'. You'll hear it all the time in shops and restaurants. Adding 제가 makes it clear you mean yourself.\n\nThe form is the same as 'shall we': 열다 → 열까요 (ㄹ stem); 닫다 → 닫을까요 (consonant); 주다 → 줄까요 (vowel). When offering something to someone you respect, use 드리다 instead of 주다: 드리 + ㄹ까요 → 드릴까요. To offer to do something for them, use ~아/어 드리다: 돕다 → 도와 드리다 → 도와 드릴까요?\n\nHow do you tell 'shall I' from 'shall we'? Context, plus clues like 제가 versus 우리 or 같이. To accept, say 네, 고마워요 or 네, 그렇게 해 주세요; to decline politely, say 아니요, 괜찮아요.", "examples": [{"ko": "창문을 열까요?", "en": "Shall I open the window?"}, {"ko": "제가 도와 드릴까요?", "en": "Shall I help you?"}, {"ko": "커피 한 잔 드릴까요?", "en": "Shall I get you a cup of coffee?"}, {"ko": "창문을 닫을까요?", "en": "Shall I close the window?"}, {"ko": "가방을 들어 드릴까요?", "en": "Shall I carry your bag for you?"}, {"ko": "제가 사진을 찍어 드릴까요?", "en": "Shall I take a picture for you?"}]}, {"point": "~ㄹ/을게(요) (I'll do it)", "explanation": "~ㄹ/을게(요) means 'I'll (do it)'. The speaker announces or promises their own action, usually in response to the listener or for their sake: offering, agreeing, promising. It's always about the speaker's own action, never about 'you' or 'he', and it's never a question.\n\nForming it: vowel stem + ㄹ게요 (하다 → 할게요; 가다 → 갈게요). Consonant stem + 을게요 (먹다 → 먹을게요; 읽다 → 읽을게요). ㄹ stems add 게요 (열다 → 열게요; 만들다 → 만들게요). Irregulars: 듣다 → 들을게요; 돕다 → 도울게요. It's pronounced as if it were 께, but always written 게.\n\nCompare with ~ㄹ/을 거예요, which just states a plan: 내일 갈 거예요 ('I'm going tomorrow', information). 내일 갈게요 ('I'll go tomorrow') has a feeling of 'okay, I'll do that, for you'. If someone asks 'Can you call me later?', answer 전화할게요, not 전화할 거예요.", "examples": [{"ko": "제가 문을 열게요.", "en": "I'll open the door."}, {"ko": "내일 다시 전화할게요.", "en": "I'll call you again tomorrow."}, {"ko": "퇴근하고 바로 갈게요.", "en": "I'll come right after I leave work."}, {"ko": "제가 도울게요.", "en": "I'll help.", "note": "돕다 is ㅂ irregular: 돕 → 도우 + ㄹ게요 → 도울게요."}, {"ko": "다음부터는 늦지 않을게요.", "en": "I won't be late from now on."}, {"ko": "이건 제가 만들게요.", "en": "I'll make this one."}]}, {"point": "~ㄹ/을까요? (do you think...?)", "explanation": "~ㄹ/을까요? can also ask for the listener's guess about something neither of you knows for sure: 'Do you think...?'. It's used for things outside the speaker's control, like the weather, other people, or how something will turn out. Unlike 'shall we' and 'shall I', the subject usually isn't the speaker.\n\nThe form is the same as before: 좋다 → 좋을까요; 오다 → 올까요; 멀다 → 멀까요 (ㄹ stem); 어렵다 → 어려울까요 (ㅂ irregular). With a noun, use 이다: 학생 + 일까요 → 학생일까요?\n\nFor something that may already have happened, put the past tense before 을까요: 퇴근하다 → 퇴근했 + 을까요 → 퇴근했을까요? ('Do you think he's already left work?'). If you're asked one of these and aren't sure, a natural reply is 글쎄요 ('Hmm, I'm not sure').", "examples": [{"ko": "내일 날씨가 좋을까요?", "en": "Do you think the weather will be nice tomorrow?"}, {"ko": "그 사람이 지금 집에 있을까요?", "en": "Do you think he's home now?"}, {"ko": "그 사람이 벌써 퇴근했을까요?", "en": "Do you think he already left work?"}, {"ko": "역까지 멀까요?", "en": "Do you think it's far to the station?"}, {"ko": "그 사람이 학생일까요?", "en": "Do you think that person is a student?"}, {"ko": "내일 시험이 어려울까요?", "en": "Do you think tomorrow's exam will be hard?", "note": "어렵다 is ㅂ irregular: 어렵 → 어려우 + ㄹ까요."}]}, {"point": "어떨까요? (how about...?)", "explanation": "어떨까요? comes from 어떻다 ('to be how') + ㄹ까요. 어떻다 is an ㅎ irregular: the ㅎ drops and ㄹ까요 attaches directly: 어떻 → 어떠 + ㄹ까요 → 어떨까요. It means 'How would it be?' or 'What do you think?'. It's softer than 어때요?, which asks more directly.\n\nTo suggest a thing, put a noun with 은/는 or 이/가 in front: 이 색깔은 어떨까요? To suggest an action, turn the verb into a noun with ~는 것, shortened to ~는 게, and add 어떨까요: 가다 → 가는 게 어떨까요? ('How about going?'). Consonant stems also just take 는: 먹다 → 먹는 게 어떨까요?\n\nBecause it asks rather than tells, it's a very polite way to suggest something to a boss or an elder. Without 요, 어떨까? is the casual version, for friends or for wondering to yourself.", "examples": [{"ko": "이 색깔은 어떨까요?", "en": "How about this color?"}, {"ko": "내일 가는 게 어떨까요?", "en": "How about going tomorrow?"}, {"ko": "사람을 더 고용하는 게 어떨까요?", "en": "What do you think about hiring more people?"}, {"ko": "택시를 타는 게 어떨까요?", "en": "How about taking a taxi?"}, {"ko": "점심으로 비빔밥은 어떨까요?", "en": "How about bibimbap for lunch?"}, {"ko": "오늘은 일찍 자는 게 어떨까?", "en": "Maybe I should go to bed early tonight?"}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "우리 어디에서 저녁을 ___?", "answer": "먹을까요", "options": ["먹을까요", "먹을게요", "먹까요", "먹을래까요"], "en": "Where shall we eat dinner?", "why": "~ㄹ/을까요? suggests doing something together; 먹다 has a consonant stem, so 을까요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "제가 가방을 들어 ___?", "answer": "드릴까요", "options": ["드릴까요", "드릴게요", "드리까요", "드를까요"], "en": "Shall I carry your bag for you?", "why": "~아/어 드릴까요? politely offers to do something for someone."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "내일 다시 ___.", "answer": "연락할게요", "options": ["연락할게요", "연락할까요", "연락하을게요", "연락할께요"], "en": "I'll contact you again tomorrow.", "why": "~ㄹ/을게요 is a promise; it is spelled 게 even though it sounds like 께."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "이 책은 제가 ___.", "answer": "읽을게요", "options": ["읽을게요", "읽게요", "읽을께요", "읽을까요"], "en": "I'll read this book.", "why": "A consonant stem takes 을게요 for 'I'll do it'."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "오늘 저녁에 뭘 ___?", "answer": "만들까", "options": ["만들까", "만들을까", "만드을까", "만들게"], "en": "What should I make tonight? (thinking to myself, casual)", "why": "An ㄹ stem just adds ㄹ까, so 만들다 becomes 만들까."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "그 사람이 벌써 집에 ___?", "answer": "도착했을까요", "options": ["도착했을까요", "도착할게요", "도착했을게요", "도착하을까요"], "en": "Do you think he has already arrived home?", "why": "~았/었을까요? guesses about something that may already have happened."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "내일 아침에 출발하는 게 ___?", "answer": "어떨까요", "options": ["어떨까요", "어떻을까요", "어떨게요", "어떠을까요"], "en": "How about leaving tomorrow morning?", "why": "어떻다 + ㄹ까요 becomes 어떨까요, a gentle way to suggest something."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "그 영화가 ___?", "answer": "재미있을까요", "options": ["재미있을까요", "재미있을게요", "재미있까요", "재미있을래요"], "en": "Do you think that movie will be fun?", "why": "~ㄹ/을까요? asks the listener's opinion about how likely something is."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Shall we take a taxi?", "ko": "택시를 탈까요?"}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Shall I turn on the light?", "ko": "불을 켤까요?"}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I'll wait here.", "ko": "여기에서 기다릴게요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "Do you think it will be cold tomorrow?", "ko": "내일 추울까요?"}], "reading": {"ko": "민수: 토요일에 같이 영화 볼까요? 지수: 좋아요. 몇 시에 만날까요? 민수: 세 시는 어떨까요? 지수: 좋아요. 그런데 표가 있을까요? 민수: 제가 오늘 밤에 표를 살게요. 지수: 고마워요. 그러면 제가 팝콘을 살게요.", "en": "Minsu: Shall we watch a movie together on Saturday? Jisu: Sounds good. What time shall we meet? Minsu: How about three o'clock? Jisu: Good. But do you think there will be tickets? Minsu: I'll buy the tickets tonight. Jisu: Thanks. Then I'll buy the popcorn.", "questions": [{"q": "민수와 지수는 토요일에 뭘 할 거예요?", "a": "같이 영화를 볼 거예요."}, {"q": "누가 표를 살 거예요?", "a": "민수가 살 거예요."}, {"q": "지수는 뭘 살 거예요?", "a": "팝콘을 살 거예요."}]}}}, {"n": 64, "title": "Lesson 64: “I am thinking of:” ~ㄹ/을까(요)", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-3-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-3-lessons-59-66/lesson-64/", "vocab": [{"ko": "물리", "en": "physics", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "분수", "en": "fountain", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "암", "en": "cancer", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "기상예보", "en": "weather forecast", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "이슬비", "en": "drizzle", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "상표", "en": "trademark", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "단풍", "en": "autumn foliage", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "재해", "en": "disaster", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "범죄", "en": "crime", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "범죄자", "en": "criminal", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "대책", "en": "measures, steps", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "횟수", "en": "number of times", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "당일", "en": "the day of", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "영화관", "en": "movie theater", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "동부", "en": "eastern part", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "남부", "en": "southern part", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "서부", "en": "western part", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "북부", "en": "northern part", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "방지하다", "en": "to prevent", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "들이마시다", "en": "to inhale", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "완료하다", "en": "to complete", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "개명하다", "en": "to change one’s name", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "지명하다", "en": "to nominate, designate", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "중복되다", "en": "to be duplicated/overlapped", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "수십", "en": "dozens of", "pos": "other"}, {"ko": "박", "en": "nights (counter)", "pos": "other"}], "grammar": [{"point": "~ㄹ/을까 하다 (I'm thinking of ...)", "explanation": "~ㄹ/을까 하다 means 'I'm thinking of ...ing' or 'I might...'. It's built from ~ㄹ/을까 ('should I...?') + 하다, so literally 'I'm going: should I...?'. It shows a plan that isn't fixed yet: you're leaning toward doing it but haven't firmly decided.\n\nForming it: vowel stem + ㄹ까 해요 (가다 → 갈까 해요; 자다 → 잘까 해요). Consonant stem + 을까 해요 (읽다 → 읽을까 해요). ㄹ stems add 까 해요 (만들다 → 만들까 해요). ㄷ irregulars: 듣다 → 들을까 해요. Only 하다 conjugates; the ~ㄹ/을까 part never changes.\n\nTo give it as a reason, connect 하다 with ~아/어서: ~ㄹ/을까 해서 = 'I was thinking of ..., so...'. Compare how sure each one sounds: ~ㄹ/을 거예요 is a firm plan, ~(으)려고 해요 is a clear intention, and ~ㄹ/을까 해요 is the most tentative.", "examples": [{"ko": "이번 주말에 단풍 구경을 갈까 해요.", "en": "I'm thinking of going to see the autumn leaves this weekend."}, {"ko": "오늘은 일찍 잘까 해요.", "en": "I'm thinking of going to bed early today."}, {"ko": "영화관에 갈까 해서 친구에게 전화했어요.", "en": "I was thinking of going to the movies, so I called a friend."}, {"ko": "이번 방학에는 한국 소설을 읽을까 해요.", "en": "I'm thinking of reading Korean novels this vacation."}, {"ko": "주말에 김밥을 만들까 해요.", "en": "I'm thinking of making gimbap this weekend."}, {"ko": "물리 수업을 들을까 해서 물어봤어요.", "en": "I was thinking of taking the physics class, so I asked about it.", "note": "듣다 is ㄷ irregular: 듣 → 들 + 을까."}]}, {"point": "~ㄹ/을까 봐(요) (I think I might ...)", "explanation": "At the end of a sentence you can swap 하다 for 보다: ~ㄹ/을까 봐요 means the same as ~ㄹ/을까 해요, 'I think I might...' or 'I'm thinking of...'. It sounds a little more casual, like a decision forming as you speak. It's used almost only in the present forms 봐 and 봐요 (보 + 아요 → 봐요).\n\nForming it: vowel stem + ㄹ까 봐요 (쉬다 → 쉴까 봐요). Consonant stem + 을까 봐요 (먹다 → 먹을까 봐요). ㄹ stems add 까 봐요 (팔다 → 팔까 봐요). 르 verbs work normally here: 자르다 → 자를까 봐요.\n\nDon't use this meaning in the middle of a sentence. To say 'I was thinking of ..., so ...', use ~ㄹ/을까 해서. In the next lesson, ~ㄹ/을까 봐 gets a second meaning, 'I'm worried that...'. The two look identical: with an action you choose to do (쉬다, 먹다), it's usually intent; with something you don't want to happen (rain, failing), it's worry.", "examples": [{"ko": "오늘은 집에서 쉴까 봐요.", "en": "I think I might rest at home today."}, {"ko": "머리를 짧게 자를까 봐요.", "en": "I'm thinking of cutting my hair short."}, {"ko": "점심에 비빔밥을 먹을까 봐요.", "en": "I think I might have bibimbap for lunch."}, {"ko": "안 입는 옷은 팔까 봐요.", "en": "I think I might sell the clothes I don't wear."}, {"ko": "주말에 단풍 구경이나 갈까 봐요.", "en": "I think I might go see the autumn leaves or something this weekend."}]}, {"point": "~ㄹ/을까 했다 (I was thinking of ..., but)", "explanation": "Put 하다 in the past, ~ㄹ/을까 했다, and you get 'I was thinking of ...ing'. It suggests you considered doing something but in the end didn't, or changed your mind. So the sentence usually continues with ~지만 or ~는데 ('but') and what actually happened.\n\nForming it: vowel stem + ㄹ까 했어요 (만나다 → 만날까 했어요). Consonant stem + 을까 했어요 (먹다 → 먹을까 했어요). ㄹ stems add 까 했어요 (열다 → 열까 했어요). To continue the sentence: 했 + 지만 → 했지만; 했 + 는데 → 했는데.\n\nCompare: 갈까 해요 = 'I'm thinking of going' (still possible). 갈까 했어요 = 'I was thinking of going' (and probably didn't). The past form carries that 'but I didn't' feeling even if you stop the sentence there.", "examples": [{"ko": "어제 친구를 만날까 했는데 비가 와서 못 만났어요.", "en": "I was thinking of meeting a friend yesterday, but it rained so I couldn't."}, {"ko": "택시를 탈까 했지만 그냥 걸어갔어요.", "en": "I thought about taking a taxi, but I just walked."}, {"ko": "이름을 바꿀까 했는데 그만뒀어요.", "en": "I was thinking of changing my name, but I gave up on it."}, {"ko": "점심을 먹을까 했지만 시간이 없었어요.", "en": "I was thinking of having lunch, but I didn't have time."}, {"ko": "집에서 쉴까 했는데 친구가 불러서 나갔어요.", "en": "I was thinking of resting at home, but a friend called me out, so I went.", "note": "부르다 is 르 irregular: 부르 + 어서 → 불러서."}, {"ko": "머리를 자를까 했어요.", "en": "I was thinking of getting a haircut (but didn't)."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "이번 방학에 제주도에 ___ 해요.", "answer": "갈까", "options": ["갈까", "가을까", "갈게", "갈까요"], "en": "I'm thinking of going to Jeju Island this vacation.", "why": "~ㄹ/을까 하다 states a tentative plan: 'I'm thinking of...'."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "오늘 저녁에는 집에서 라면을 ___ 해요.", "answer": "먹을까", "options": ["먹을까", "먹까", "먹을게", "먹으까"], "en": "I'm thinking of eating ramen at home this evening.", "why": "A consonant stem takes 을까 before 하다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "새 옷을 살까 ___ 백화점에 갔어요.", "answer": "해서", "options": ["해서", "하서", "하고", "했어서"], "en": "I was thinking of buying new clothes, so I went to the department store.", "why": "Mid-sentence, use ~ㄹ/을까 해서 to mean 'I was thinking of..., so'."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "저녁에 공원에서 ___ 봐요.", "answer": "걸을까", "options": ["걸을까", "걷을까", "걸까", "걷까"], "en": "I think I might take a walk in the park this evening.", "why": "걷다 is ㄷ irregular, so it becomes 걸을까."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "어제 친구를 ___ 했는데 너무 피곤해서 그냥 집에 있었어요.", "answer": "만날까", "options": ["만날까", "만나을까", "만나까", "만날게"], "en": "I was thinking of meeting a friend yesterday, but I was so tired I just stayed home.", "why": "~ㄹ/을까 했다 means you considered it but didn't do it."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "버스를 탈까 ___ 날씨가 좋아서 걸어갔어요.", "answer": "했는데", "options": ["했는데", "해서", "하면", "할게요"], "en": "I was thinking of taking the bus, but the weather was nice so I walked.", "why": "Past 했는데 shows a plan you considered and then dropped."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "작년에 ___ 했지만 안 했어요.", "answer": "개명할까", "options": ["개명할까", "개명하을까", "개명할게", "개명해을까"], "en": "Last year I was thinking of changing my name, but I didn't.", "why": "A vowel stem takes ㄹ까 before 하다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "이번 겨울에는 운동을 ___ 해요.", "answer": "시작할까", "options": ["시작할까", "시작하을까", "시작할게", "시작해까"], "en": "I'm thinking of starting to exercise this winter.", "why": "시작하 + ㄹ까 해요 states a tentative plan."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I'm thinking of going to the movie theater tonight.", "ko": "오늘 밤에 영화관에 갈까 해요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I think I might stay two nights in Busan.", "ko": "부산에서 2박을 할까 봐요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I was thinking of calling my mother, but it was too late.", "ko": "어머니께 전화할까 했는데 너무 늦었어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I'm thinking of looking at the weather forecast first.", "ko": "먼저 기상예보를 볼까 해요."}], "reading": {"ko": "이번 가을에 단풍 구경을 갈까 해요. 처음에는 북부에 있는 큰 산에 갈까 했는데 너무 멀어요. 그래서 남부에 있는 산에 갈까 봐요. 거기에서 1박을 할까 해요. 그런데 기상예보에서 주말에 이슬비가 온다고 했어요. 그래도 저는 우산을 가지고 갈 거예요. 단풍을 보면서 사진을 많이 찍고 싶어요.", "en": "This autumn I'm thinking of going to see the autumn leaves. At first I was thinking of going to a big mountain in the north, but it's too far. So I think I might go to a mountain in the south. I'm thinking of staying one night there. But the weather forecast said it will drizzle on the weekend. Even so, I'll take an umbrella and go. I want to take lots of photos while looking at the leaves.", "questions": [{"q": "이 사람은 이번 가을에 뭘 할까 해요?", "a": "단풍 구경을 갈까 해요."}, {"q": "왜 북부에 있는 산에 안 가요?", "a": "너무 멀어서 안 가요."}, {"q": "기상예보에서 뭐라고 했어요?", "a": "주말에 이슬비가 온다고 했어요."}]}}}, {"n": 65, "title": "Lesson 65: I am worried about: ㄹ/을까 봐", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-3-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-3-lessons-59-66/lesson-65/", "vocab": [{"ko": "호랑이", "en": "tiger", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "역사가", "en": "historian", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "집주인", "en": "landlord", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "다과", "en": "refreshments", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "작업", "en": "work, operations", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "예산", "en": "budget", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "소독약", "en": "disinfectant", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "기구", "en": "apparatus, equipment", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "지붕", "en": "roof", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "한구석", "en": "corner, nook", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "가로", "en": "width", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "세로", "en": "height, vertical length (vs 가로 width)", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "논문", "en": "thesis", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "파도", "en": "waves", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "총", "en": "gun", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "찾아내다", "en": "to find out, to discover", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "쏘다", "en": "to shoot, to sting", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "방해하다", "en": "to distract", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "소독하다", "en": "to disinfect", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "대단하다", "en": "to be great, tremendous", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "화려하다", "en": "to be fancy, splendid", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "엷다", "en": "to be light (in shade)", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "무례하다", "en": "to be rude", "pos": "adjective"}, {"ko": "오래 전", "en": "a long time before", "pos": "other"}, {"ko": "글쎄", "en": "well... / let me see", "pos": "other"}], "grammar": [{"point": "~ㄹ/을까 봐 (걱정되다) (I'm worried that ...)", "explanation": "~ㄹ/을까 봐 걱정되다 means 'I'm worried that ... might happen'. It combines ~ㄹ/을까 ('I wonder if...') with 보다, so the idea is 'thinking it might..., I'm worried'. Use it for something bad or unwanted that could happen.\n\nForming it: vowel stem + ㄹ까 봐 (떨어지다 → 떨어질까 봐). Consonant stem + 을까 봐 (늦다 → 늦을까 봐). ㄹ stems add 까 봐 (멀다 → 멀까 봐). ㅂ irregulars: 춥다 → 추울까 봐. For something that may already have happened, use the past: 잃다 → 잃었 + 을까 봐 → 잃었을까 봐.\n\n걱정되다 or 걱정이다 are the usual endings for your own worry; 걱정하다 is common when describing someone else's worry (엄마는 제가 아플까 봐 걱정하세요). In conversation, the 걱정 part is often dropped: 비가 올까 봐요. In lesson 64, ~ㄹ/을까 봐요 meant 'I think I might...'. If the thing is unwanted and outside your control, it's worry; if it's an action you choose, it's intent.", "examples": [{"ko": "시험에 떨어질까 봐 걱정돼요.", "en": "I'm worried I'll fail the exam."}, {"ko": "예산이 부족할까 봐 걱정돼요.", "en": "I'm worried the budget won't be enough."}, {"ko": "아이가 길을 잃었을까 봐 걱정돼요.", "en": "I'm worried the child might have gotten lost."}, {"ko": "비가 올까 봐요.", "en": "I'm worried it might rain."}, {"ko": "내일 날씨가 추울까 봐 걱정돼요.", "en": "I'm worried it'll be cold tomorrow.", "note": "춥다 is ㅂ irregular: 춥 → 추우 + ㄹ까 봐."}, {"ko": "엄마는 제가 아플까 봐 걱정하세요.", "en": "My mom worries that I might get sick."}]}, {"point": "~ㄹ/을까 봐 + clause (worried that ..., so ...)", "explanation": "Instead of ending with 걱정되다, you can follow ~ㄹ/을까 봐 with another clause that says what you did, or didn't do, because of the worry: 'worried that..., I ...' or 'in case...'. 늦을까 봐 택시를 탔어요 = 'I was worried I'd be late, so I took a taxi'.\n\nThe tense goes on the last verb only. The ~ㄹ/을까 봐 part stays the same whether the sentence is past or present: 늦을까 봐 택시를 탔어요 (past) and 늦을까 봐 택시를 타요 (habit). Don't change it to 늦었을까 봐 for a past story; ~았/었을까 봐 is only for worrying that something has already happened.\n\n봐 can also be 봐서 here, with the same meaning: 늦을까 봐서. The second clause explains what you did. Don't follow it with a command or suggestion, such as 'take an umbrella'; it's for explaining your own reasons.", "examples": [{"ko": "늦을까 봐 택시를 탔어요.", "en": "I was worried I'd be late, so I took a taxi."}, {"ko": "비가 올까 봐 우산을 가져왔어요.", "en": "I brought an umbrella in case it rained."}, {"ko": "아기가 깰까 봐 조용히 말했어요.", "en": "I spoke quietly so the baby wouldn't wake up."}, {"ko": "잊어버릴까 봐 메모를 했어요.", "en": "I wrote a note in case I forgot."}, {"ko": "살이 찔까 봐 밤에는 안 먹어요.", "en": "I don't eat at night because I'm worried about gaining weight."}, {"ko": "길이 막힐까 봐서 일찍 출발했어요.", "en": "I left early in case the roads were jammed."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "회의에 ___ 봐 걱정돼요.", "answer": "늦을까", "options": ["늦을까", "늦까", "늦을게", "늦었을게"], "en": "I'm worried I'll be late for the meeting.", "why": "~ㄹ/을까 봐 걱정되다 means 'I'm worried that...'."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "날씨가 ___ 봐 따뜻한 옷을 입었어요.", "answer": "추울까", "options": ["추울까", "춥을까", "추을까", "춥까"], "en": "I was worried it would be cold, so I wore warm clothes.", "why": "춥다 is ㅂ irregular, so it becomes 추울까."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "길을 ___ 봐 지도를 가져갔어요.", "answer": "잃을까", "options": ["잃을까", "잃었을까", "잃을게", "일을까"], "en": "I was worried I'd get lost, so I took a map.", "why": "The tense goes on the final verb, so the verb before ~ㄹ/을까 봐 stays non-past."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "친구가 제 문자를 못 ___ 봐 걱정돼요.", "answer": "봤을까", "options": ["봤을까", "볼게", "봤을게", "보을까"], "en": "I'm worried my friend might not have seen my text.", "why": "For something that may already have happened, use ~았/었을까 봐."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "동생이 ___ 봐 불을 끄고 조용히 나갔어요.", "answer": "깰까", "options": ["깰까", "깨을까", "깰게", "깨까"], "en": "I was worried my younger sibling would wake up, so I turned off the light and quietly went out.", "why": "A vowel stem takes ㄹ까 봐."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "내일 눈이 많이 ___ 봐요.", "answer": "올까", "options": ["올까", "오을까", "올게", "왔을게"], "en": "I'm worried it might snow a lot tomorrow.", "why": "~ㄹ/을까 봐요 alone can mean worry, with 걱정되다 dropped."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "제 말이 ___ 봐 걱정했어요.", "answer": "무례했을까", "options": ["무례했을까", "무례할게", "무례하을까", "무례했을게"], "en": "I was worried my words might have been rude.", "why": "~았/었을까 봐 worries about something that may already be true."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "집주인이 우리를 무례하다고 ___ 봐 걱정돼요.", "answer": "생각할까", "options": ["생각할까", "생각하을까", "생각할게", "생각했을게"], "en": "I'm worried the landlord will think we're rude.", "why": "생각하 + ㄹ까 봐 says what you fear might happen."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I'm worried the bus might not come.", "ko": "버스가 안 올까 봐 걱정돼요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I studied hard because I was worried I'd fail the exam.", "ko": "시험에 떨어질까 봐 열심히 공부했어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I'm worried the roof might break.", "ko": "지붕이 부서질까 봐 걱정돼요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I closed the window because I was worried my younger sibling would catch a cold.", "ko": "동생이 감기에 걸릴까 봐 창문을 닫았어요."}], "reading": {"ko": "저는 요즘 논문을 쓰고 있어요. 다음 달까지 논문을 끝내야 돼요. 그런데 시간이 부족할까 봐 걱정돼요. 그래서 주말에도 도서관에서 작업해요. 어제는 친구가 제 논문을 읽어 줬어요. 친구가 '글쎄요, 조금 길어요.'라고 했어요. 그래서 논문을 조금 짧게 바꿨어요.", "en": "These days I'm writing my thesis. I have to finish it by next month. But I'm worried there won't be enough time. So I work in the library even on weekends. Yesterday my friend read my thesis for me. My friend said, 'Well... it's a bit long.' So I made the thesis a little shorter.", "questions": [{"q": "이 사람은 왜 주말에도 도서관에서 작업해요?", "a": "시간이 부족할까 봐 주말에도 작업해요."}, {"q": "친구는 논문이 어떻다고 했어요?", "a": "조금 길다고 했어요."}, {"q": "이 사람은 논문을 어떻게 바꿨어요?", "a": "조금 짧게 바꿨어요."}]}}}, {"n": 66, "title": "Lesson 66: Almost: 거의 and ~ㄹ/을 뻔 했다", "url": "https://www.howtostudykorean.com/unit-3-intermediate-korean-grammar/unit-3-lessons-59-66/lesson-66/", "vocab": [{"ko": "반항아", "en": "a rebel", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "귀신", "en": "ghost", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "거미", "en": "spider", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "고래", "en": "whale", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "늑대", "en": "wolf", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "학위", "en": "academic degree", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "학사 학위", "en": "bachelor’s degree", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "석사 학위", "en": "master’s degree", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "박사 학위", "en": "Ph.D.", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "지식", "en": "knowledge", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "비평", "en": "criticism", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "등급", "en": "rating, grade", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "단추", "en": "button", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "화장품", "en": "cosmetics", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "소화기", "en": "fire extinguisher", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "분필", "en": "chalk", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "동기", "en": "motive", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "일상", "en": "everyday life", "pos": "noun"}, {"ko": "유의하다", "en": "to pay attention to", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "정지하다", "en": "to stop, to halt", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "모방하다", "en": "to imitate, to mimic", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "반항하다", "en": "to rebel", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "보장하다", "en": "to guarantee (ensure: rights, safety)", "pos": "verb"}, {"ko": "어긋나다", "en": "to be out of line/step", "pos": "verb"}], "grammar": [{"point": "거의 (almost)", "explanation": "거의 means 'almost' or 'nearly'. It goes right before the word or phrase it describes.\n\nWith actions, it shows something has nearly reached its end point, often together with 다 ('all'): 거의 다 했어요 ('I've almost finished'), 거의 다 왔어요 ('we're almost there'). Here most of the action has already happened.\n\nWith descriptions and amounts it means 'nearly' or 'just about': 거의 같아요 ('almost the same'), 거의 매일 ('almost every day'). With a negative, it means 'hardly' or 'almost never': 거의 안 먹어요 ('I hardly eat it'), 거의 없어요 ('there's hardly any').\n\nDon't use 거의 alone for something that almost happened but never started, like 'I almost fell'. That's the job of ~ㄹ/을 뻔했다, the next point.", "examples": [{"ko": "숙제를 거의 다 했어요.", "en": "I've almost finished my homework."}, {"ko": "거의 다 왔어요.", "en": "We're almost there."}, {"ko": "이 두 가방은 거의 같아요.", "en": "These two bags are almost the same."}, {"ko": "요즘은 거의 매일 운동해요.", "en": "These days I exercise almost every day."}, {"ko": "저는 고기를 거의 안 먹어요.", "en": "I hardly eat meat."}, {"ko": "냉장고에 음식이 거의 없어요.", "en": "There's hardly any food in the fridge."}]}, {"point": "~ㄹ/을 뻔했다 (nearly happened)", "explanation": "~ㄹ/을 뻔했다 means 'almost did' or 'nearly happened': something came very close to happening but didn't. Usually it's something bad you just avoided: nearly falling, nearly missing a flight, nearly forgetting. Because it describes a near miss, it's almost always past tense: 뻔했어요.\n\nForming it: vowel stem + ㄹ 뻔했어요 (넘어지다 → 넘어질 뻔했어요). Consonant stem + 을 뻔했어요 (잊다 → 잊을 뻔했어요). ㄹ stems drop nothing and add 뻔했어요 (울다 → 울 뻔했어요). Note the space before 뻔.\n\nCompare with 거의: 거의 다 했어요 means most of the action happened. ~ㄹ/을 뻔했다 means it didn't happen at all, but nearly did. 거의 can be added for emphasis (거의 넘어질 뻔했어요) but isn't needed. In casual speech, 죽을 뻔했어요 is a common exaggeration: 'I nearly died' (of laughter, fear or tiredness).", "examples": [{"ko": "계단에서 넘어질 뻔했어요.", "en": "I almost fell on the stairs."}, {"ko": "늦잠을 자서 비행기를 놓칠 뻔했어요.", "en": "I overslept and nearly missed my flight."}, {"ko": "거미를 보고 소리를 지를 뻔했어요.", "en": "I saw a spider and almost screamed."}, {"ko": "차에 거의 부딪칠 뻔했어요.", "en": "I very nearly hit the car."}, {"ko": "친구와의 약속을 잊을 뻔했어요.", "en": "I almost forgot my plans with my friend."}, {"ko": "영화가 너무 슬퍼서 울 뻔했어요.", "en": "The movie was so sad I almost cried.", "note": "울다 is an ㄹ stem, so it just adds 뻔: 울 뻔했어요."}]}], "workbook": {"exercises": [{"type": "fill", "ko": "편지를 ___ 다 썼어요.", "answer": "거의", "options": ["거의", "거이", "뻔", "별로"], "en": "I've almost finished writing the letter.", "why": "거의 다 means 'almost all', for an action nearly finished."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "아침에 버스를 ___ 뻔했어요.", "answer": "놓칠", "options": ["놓칠", "놓친", "놓치는", "놓쳤을"], "en": "I almost missed the bus this morning.", "why": "뻔했다 always follows the future modifier ~ㄹ/을."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "너무 아파서 ___ 뻔했어요.", "answer": "울", "options": ["울", "울을", "운", "우는"], "en": "It hurt so much that I almost cried.", "why": "An ㄹ stem already ends in ㄹ, so 울다 becomes 울 뻔했어요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "늦잠을 자서 수업에 늦을 ___.", "answer": "뻔했어요", "options": ["뻔했어요", "뻔이었어요", "뻔있었어요", "뻔하겠어요"], "en": "I overslept and was almost late for class.", "why": "~ㄹ/을 뻔하다 is almost always used in the past: 뻔했어요."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "음식이 너무 매워서 ___ 뻔했어요.", "answer": "죽을", "options": ["죽을", "죽는", "죽은", "죽었을"], "en": "The food was so spicy I almost died.", "why": "A consonant stem takes 을 before 뻔했다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "빨간불에 길을 ___ 뻔했어요.", "answer": "건널", "options": ["건널", "건넌", "건너", "건넜을"], "en": "I almost crossed the road on a red light.", "why": "건너다 has a vowel stem, so it takes ㄹ before 뻔했다."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "언니와 저는 키가 ___ 같아요.", "answer": "거의", "options": ["거의", "전혀", "별로", "뻔"], "en": "My older sister and I are almost the same height.", "why": "거의 같다 means 'almost the same'; 전혀 and 별로 need a negative."}, {"type": "fill", "ko": "셔츠 단추가 ___ 뻔했어요.", "answer": "떨어질", "options": ["떨어질", "떨어진", "떨어지는", "떨어져"], "en": "The button on my shirt almost came off.", "why": "~ㄹ/을 뻔했다 says something almost happened but didn't."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I've almost finished my coffee.", "ko": "커피를 거의 다 마셨어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I almost forgot my friend's birthday.", "ko": "친구 생일을 잊을 뻔했어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I almost lost my wallet.", "ko": "지갑을 잃어버릴 뻔했어요."}, {"type": "translate", "en": "I almost fell asleep in class.", "ko": "수업 중에 잠이 들 뻔했어요."}], "reading": {"ko": "어제는 정말 힘든 날이었어요. 아침에 늦잠을 자서 지하철을 놓칠 뻔했어요. 다행히 회사에 늦지 않았어요. 점심에는 셔츠 단추가 떨어질 뻔했어요. 저녁에 집에 갈 때는 계단에서 넘어질 뻔했어요. 집에 도착했을 때 거의 열한 시였어요. 저는 씻고 바로 잤어요.", "en": "Yesterday was a really hard day. I overslept in the morning and almost missed the subway. Fortunately I wasn't late for work. At lunch my shirt button almost came off. In the evening, on my way home, I almost fell on the stairs. When I got home it was almost eleven o'clock. I washed up and went straight to bed.", "questions": [{"q": "아침에 왜 지하철을 놓칠 뻔했어요?", "a": "늦잠을 자서 놓칠 뻔했어요."}, {"q": "회사에 늦었어요?", "a": "아니요, 늦지 않았어요."}, {"q": "계단에서 무슨 일이 있었어요?", "a": "넘어질 뻔했어요."}]}}}]);
